ulations L46:     1. cite36†A.1 Moments and characteristic functions L47:     2. cite37†A.2 Oscillator entropy bound L48:     3. cite38†A.3 The heterodyne Fourier identity L49:     4. cite39†A.4 Integral maps and finite instrument entropies L50:     5. cite40†A.5 Minkowski’s determinant inequality L51:   9. cite41†B Gaussian characterization from independent outputs L52:   10. cite42†References L53: cite43†License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L54: \nL55: arXiv:2609.40061v1 [quant-ph] 30 Sep 2026\nL56: # Equality in the Bosonic Quantum Entropy Power Inequality\nL57: \nL58: Yonglong Li Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics Affiliation: Xi’an Jiaotong University Email: liyonglong@xjtu.edu.cn\nL59: ###### Abstract\nL60: The bosonic quantum entropy power inequality bounds the entropy of a beam-splitter output in terms of the input entropies. We determine its complete equality class among independent inputs of finite mean photon number: for any number of modes and any transmissivity strictly between zero and one, equality in either the linear or the exponential form holds if and only if the inputs are Gaussian with the same covariance matrix, allowing arbitrary displacements.\nL61: The main step shows that equality for one output forces the two outputs to be independent: a thermal auxiliary converts equality into preservation of mutual information by a fixed noisy channel, and a two-outcome instrument shows that preservation of mutual information requires a product state with the reference. The quantum Darmois–Skitovich theorem then gives Gaussianity, while a central-limit argument reduces exponential equality to linear equality.\nL62: Keywords: Quantum entropy power inequality, Gaussian states, beam splitter, mutual information, equality conditions.\nL63: ## 1 Introduction\nL64: \nL65: Consider two independent $m$-mode bosonic systems, $A$ and $B$, mixed on a beam splitter of transmissivity $0<\\eta<1$. If $R_{X}$ denotes the vector of position and momentum quadratures of system $X$, the outputs are\nL66: \nL67:  | $\\displaystyle R_{C}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sqrt{\\eta}R_{A}+\\sqrt{1-\\eta}R_{B},$  |\nL68:  | $\\displaystyle R_{E}$  | $\\displaystyle=-\\sqrt{1-\\eta}R_{A}+\\sqrt{\\eta}R_{B}.$  |  | (1)\nL69: The quantum entropy power inequality (qEPI) lower-bounds the entropy of the retained output $C$:\nL70: \nL71:  | $\\displaystyle S(C)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\eta S(A)+(1-\\eta)S(B),$  |  | (2)\nL72:  | $\\displaystyle e^{S(C)/m}$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\eta e^{S(A)/m}+(1-\\eta)e^{S(B)/m}.$  |  | (3)\nL73: \nL74: Here $S$ is the von Neumann entropy, with natural logarithms. We ask: which independent inputs make either bound exact?\nL75: König and Smith [cite44†1 , cite45†2 ] proved (cite46†2 ) for every transmissivity and (cite47†3 ) for a balanced beam splitter. De Palma, Mari, and Giovannetti [cite48†3 ] proved the exponential inequality for every transmissivity. The extension to several independent inputs under general linear mixing is due to De Palma, Mari, Lloyd, and Giovannetti [cite49†4 ]. These results provide the inequalities but not a complete finite-energy equality characterization. De Palma et al.\nL76: observed that Gaussian inputs with proportional covariance matrices fail to attain the exponential bound unless their entropies agree [cite48†3 , discussion following Eq. (5)]. We establish the complete characterization here.\nL77: Equality questions distinguish this quantum problem from its classical counterpart. For independent random vectors with finite covariance matrices and finite differential entropies, equality in the classical EPI of Shannon and Stam [cite50†5 , cite51†6 ] characterizes Gaussian inputs with proportional covariances. The linear-entropy form requires identical covariances [cite52†7 , Sec. II-E1]. In the quantum problem, both forms require identical covariance matrices.\nL78: ### 1.1 Main results\nL79: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquality in the Bosonic Quantum Entropy Power Inequality (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.40061v1)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":230}); Total lines: 812\nL202:  | $\\displaystyle\\chi_{CE}(u,v)={}$  | $\\displaystyle\\chi_{A}(\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,u-\\sqrt{1-\\eta}\\,v)\\chi_{B}(\\sqrt{1-\\eta}\\,u+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,v).$  |  | (18)\nL203: \nL204: The same mixing formula holds for complex arguments. In particular $V_{C}=\\eta V_{A}+(1-\\eta)V_{B}$.\nL205: ### 2.3 The affine deficit\nL206: \nL207: The proof uses a variational weight $p$, distinct from the physical transmissivity $\\eta$. For $0\\leq p\\leq 1$ and $0<q<1$, let\nL208: \nL209:  | $$d(p\\|q)=p\\log\\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\\log\\frac{1-p}{1-q}.$$  |  | (19)\nL210: \nL211: For independent inputs define\nL212: \nL213:  | $\\displaystyle F_{p}(A,B)$  | $\\displaystyle=S(C)-pS(A)-(1-p)S(B)+md(p\\|\\eta),$  |  | (20)\nL214:  | $\\displaystyle L_{\\eta}(A,B)$  | $\\displaystyle=F_{\\eta}(A,B)=L_{C}.$  |  | (21)\nL215: For a joint state $\\rho_{ABM}$, with $\\rho_{CM}=(\\mathcal{B}_{\\eta}\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{M})(\\rho_{ABM})$, define\nL216: \nL217:  | $\\displaystyle F_{p}(A,B\\mid M)={}$  | $\\displaystyle S(C\\mid M)-pS(A\\mid M)-(1-p)S(B\\mid M)+md(p\\|\\eta).$  |  | (22)\nL218: \nL219: Write $L_{\\eta}(A,B\\mid M)=F_{\\eta}(A,B\\mid M)$ for the conditional linear deficit. The conditional qEPI [cite66†17 , Theorem 6, Eq. (106)] states that\nL220: \nL221:  | $$I(A;B\\mid M)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad F_{p}(A,B\\mid M)\\geq 0$$  |  | (23)\nL222: for every $p\\in[0,1]$, provided the bosonic inputs have finite energy and $S(M)<\\infty$. A one-dimensional memory gives the unconditional inequality. Below the memory consists of finite-energy bosonic modes.\nL223: Table 1: Notation used in the proof\nL224: Symbol  | Meaning\nL225: --- | ---\nL226: $\\eta,\\ p$  | Physical transmissivity and variational weight\nL227: $R,\\Omega;\\ d,V$  | Quadratures, symplectic form; mean and covariance\nL228: $H_{m},\\ \\tau_{N}$  | Photon number and thermal state with $N$ photons per mode\nL229: $L_{C},L_{E};\\ F_{p}$  | Two output deficits; affine deficit\nL230: $M=A_{+}B_{+}$  | Memory in the doubling identity\nL231: $\\Xi$  | Final four-output state of the doubled circuit\nL232: $\\Phi$  | Thermal attenuator of transmissivity $1/2$ with environment $\\tau_{2}$\nL233: $G,\\ F$  | Vacuum-branch effect and binary flag\nL234: $J=E_{+},\\ \\sigma_{CJ}$  | Retained noisy reference and noisy joint state\nL235: $\\rho^{\\boxplus n}$  | $n$-fold quantum convolution of $\\rho$\nL236: ## 3 Proof of linear equality\nL237: \nL238: We first establish an entropy decomposition and a strict information-loss property. Their combination will prove Theorem cite59†1 .\nL239: ### 3.1 Conditional doubling\nL240: \nL241: Start with four independent finite-energy $m$-mode input systems in the state\nL242: \nL243:  | $$\\rho_{A_{1}}\\otimes\\rho_{B_{1}}\\otimes\\rho_{A_{2}}\\otimes\\rho_{B_{2}}.$$  |  | (24)\nL244: \nL245: The two pairs may be different. Mix $A_{1},A_{2}$ on a balanced beam splitter, and independently mix $B_{1},B_{2}$ in the same way:\nL246: \nL247:  | $$R_{A_{+}}=\\frac{R_{A_{1}}+R_{A_{2}}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\qquad R_{A_{-}}=\\frac{-R_{A_{1}}+R_{A_{2}}}{\\sqrt{2}},$$  |  | (25)\nL248: with analogous definitions for $B$. We call these two balanced beam splitters the copy step. Then apply the $\\eta$-beam splitter $U_{\\eta}$ to $A_{+}B_{+}$ and to $A_{-}B_{-}$, producing $C_{+}E_{+}$ and $C_{-}E_{-}$. Set $M=A_{+}B_{+}$ before converting it unitarily to $C_{+}E_{+}$. To specify the states underlying these labels, write $\\zeta$ for the state after the copy step:\nL249:  | $\\displaystyle\\zeta_{A_{+}A_{-}B_{+}B_{-}}={}$  | $\\displaystyle\\bigl[U_{1/2}(\\rho_{A_{1}}\\otimes\\rho_{A_{2}})U_{1/2}^{\\dagger}\\bigr]\\otimes\\bigl[U_{1/2}(\\rho_{B_{1}}\\otimes\\rho_{B_{2}})U_{1/2}^{\\dagger}\\bigr].$  |  | (26)\nL250: Tensor factors are reordered according to their labels. Applying $\\mathcal{B}_{\\eta}$ to the minus pair and retaining $M$ gives a state on $C_{-}M$. Applying the $\\eta$-beam splitter to $M$ then gives the same state on $C_{-}C_{+}E_{+}$. The latter is a marginal of the four-output state, denoted $\\Xi_{C_{+}E_{+}C_{-}E_{-}}$. The labels $M$ and $C_{+}E_{+}$ describe the memory before and after its $\\eta$-beam splitter, respectively.\nL251: This construction is a quantum version of the doubling trick used in classical Gaussian extremality arguments [cite63†15 ]. Here the conditional qEPI provides the nonnegative remainder. Mixing two input pairs separates their total deficit into the deficit of the plus pair, the deficit of the minus pair conditioned on the plus inputs, and a correlation term. The following identity makes all three terms nonnegative.\nL252: Allowing the two input pairs to differ will let us choose a thermal auxiliary in Section cite20†3.4 .\nL253: ###### Lemma 3 (Doubling with different pairs).\nL254: \nL255: Let $\\zeta_{A_{-}B_{-}M}$ be the state in (cite79†26 ), with tensor factors reordered and $M=A_{+}B_{+}$. Define\nL256: \nL257:  | $$\\zeta_{C_{-}M}=(\\mathcal{B}_{\\eta}\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{M})(\\zeta_{A_{-}B_{-}M}).$$  |\nL258: \nL259: The conditional deficit below uses these two states; the CMI is evaluated in the four-output state $\\Xi$. For each fixed $p\\in[0,1]$,\nL260:  |  | $\\displaystyle F_{p}(A_{1},B_{1})+F_{p}(A_{2},B_{2})=F_{p}(A_{+},B_{+})+F_{p}(A_{-},B_{-}\\mid M)+I(C_{-};E_{+}\\mid C_{+}).$  |  | (27)\nL261: \nL262: All three terms on the right are nonnegative.\nL263: ###### Proof.\nL264: \nL265: The product in (cite79†26 ) proves independence between the entire $A$-group and the entire $B$-group. Indeed,\nL266: \nL267:  | $\\displaystyle S(A_{-}\\mid A_{+}B_{+})=S(A_{-}A_{+})+S(B_{+})-S(A_{+})-S(B_{+})=S(A_{-}\\mid A_{+}).$  |  | (28)\nL268: \nL269: Similarly $S(B_{-}\\mid A_{+}B_{+})=S(B_{-}\\mid B_{+})$. Moreover\nL270: \nL271:  | $$S(A_{-}B_{-}\\mid A_{+}B_{+})=S(A_{-}\\mid A_{+})+S(B_{-}\\mid B_{+}),$$  |  | (29)\nL272: \nL273: which verifies conditional independence explicitly. Hence\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquality in the Bosonic Quantum Entropy Power Inequality (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.40061v1)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":430}); Total lines: 812\nL412: Let $\\zeta^{0}_{YK}=(\\Psi_{0}\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{K})(\\omega^{0}_{X^{\\prime}K})$ and $\\zeta^{1}_{YK}=|0\\rangle\\!\\langle 0|_{Y}\\otimes\\omega_{K}^{1}$. Both transformations preserve the corresponding $K$ marginal. The state with the flag retained is $\\zeta_{FYK}=\\sum_{f}p_{f}|f\\rangle\\langle f|_{F}\\otimes\\zeta^{f}_{YK}$.\nL413: \nL414: All branch and joint entropies below are finite, as verified in Appendix cite39†A.4 . Thus ordinary entropy chain rules apply.\nL415: Data processing for $\\mathcal{M}$, followed by the chain rule for a classical flag, gives\nL416: \nL417:  | $\\displaystyle I(X;K)_{\\omega}$  | $\\displaystyle\\overset{\\mathrm{(a)}}{\\geq}I(FX^{\\prime};K)_{(\\mathcal{M}\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{K})(\\omega)}$  |\nL418:  |  | $\\displaystyle=I(F;K)+p_{1}I(X^{\\prime};K)_{\\omega^{1}}+p_{0}I(X^{\\prime};K)_{\\omega^{0}}.$  |  | (57)\nL419: \nL420: On the other hand, discarding the flag and then using data processing for $\\Psi_{0}$ gives\nL421:  | $\\displaystyle I(Y;K)_{\\zeta}$  | $\\displaystyle\\overset{\\mathrm{(b)}}{\\leq}I(FY;K)_{\\zeta}$  |\nL422:  |  | $\\displaystyle=I(F;K)+p_{0}I(Y;K)_{\\zeta^{0}}$  |\nL423:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\overset{\\mathrm{(c)}}{\\leq}I(F;K)+p_{0}I(X^{\\prime};K)_{\\omega^{0}}.$  |  | (58)\nL424: Steps (a), (b), and (c) apply data processing to the instrument, discarding the flag, and the channel $\\Psi_{0}$, respectively. The $FK$ marginal is the same in both displays, and the $f=1$ term vanishes in the second display because $\\zeta^{1}$ is a product. Subtracting yields\nL425: \nL426:  | $$I(X;K)_{\\omega}-I(Y;K)_{\\zeta}\\geq p_{1}I(X^{\\prime};K)_{\\omega^{1}}.$$  |  | (59)\nL427: If the left side is zero, then $\\omega^{1}=\\omega^{1}_{X^{\\prime}}\\otimes\\omega^{1}_{K}$. Taking its $X^{\\prime}$ marginal in (cite95†56 ) therefore gives\nL428: \nL429:  | $$(G^{1/2}\\otimes I_{K})\\omega_{XK}(G^{1/2}\\otimes I_{K})=(G^{1/2}\\omega_{X}G^{1/2})\\otimes\\omega_{K}^{1}.$$  |  | (60)\nL430: For number-basis vectors $|\\bm{n}\\rangle,|\\bm{n}^{\\prime}\\rangle$, their eigenvalues $g_{\\bm{n}}=3^{-m}2^{-\\sum_{j}n_{j}}$ and $g_{\\bm{n}^{\\prime}}$ are positive. Taking the corresponding operator block of (cite96†60 ) and cancelling $\\sqrt{g_{\\bm{n}}g_{\\bm{n}^{\\prime}}}$ gives\nL431: \nL432:  | $$\\langle\\bm{n}|\\omega_{XK}|\\bm{n}^{\\prime}\\rangle=\\langle\\bm{n}|\\omega_{X}|\\bm{n}^{\\prime}\\rangle\\,\\omega_{K}^{1}.$$  |\nL433: Equality of all blocks implies $\\omega_{XK}=\\omega_{X}\\otimes\\omega_{K}^{1}$. Taking the partial trace over $X$ identifies $\\omega_{K}^{1}=\\omega_{K}$. The converse holds because a local channel preserves product states. ∎\nL434: ###### Remark 7.\nL435: The filter $G^{1/2}$ is injective, although its inverse is unbounded. The number-basis argument above uses only its positive eigenvalues; it does not apply the inverse to a trace-class operator. This distinction is useful when seeking quantitative versions of (cite97†59 ). We also note why the standard equality condition for data processing does not suffice here.\nL436: By Petz’s theorem [cite98†31 , cite99†32 ], equality in (cite100†55 ) implies that $\\omega_{XK}$ is recovered from $\\zeta_{YK}$ by a local channel on $Y$; since $\\Phi$ is entanglement breaking, this only shows that $\\omega_{XK}$ is separable, whereas the instrument argument yields a product.\nL437: ### 3.4 From equality to independent outputs\nL438: \nL439: ###### Proof of (i)$\\Rightarrow$(ii) in Theorem cite59†1 .\nL440: \nL441: Let $\\omega_{CE}=U_{\\eta}(\\rho_{A}\\otimes\\rho_{B})U_{\\eta}^{\\dagger}$ and assume $L_{\\eta}(A,B)=0$.\nL442: ##### 1. Add a thermal equality pair.\nL443: \nL444: In Lemma cite70†3 , set\nL445: \nL446:  | $$(\\rho_{A_{1}},\\rho_{B_{1}})=(\\rho_{A},\\rho_{B}),\\qquad(\\rho_{A_{2}},\\rho_{B_{2}})=(\\tau_{2},\\tau_{2}).$$  |  | (61)\nL447: \nL448: All four inputs are independent. If the original energies are $\\mathsf{E}_{A},\\mathsf{E}_{B}$, the total energy in this circuit is $\\mathsf{E}_{A}+\\mathsf{E}_{B}+4m$. Every beam splitter preserves that total, so the energy bound (cite101†9 ) makes all circuit entropies finite. The auxiliary state has the form\nL449:  | $$(\\tau_{2})_{A_{2}}\\otimes(\\tau_{2})_{B_{2}}=3^{-2m}\\exp[-\\log(3/2)(H_{A_{2}}+H_{B_{2}})].$$  |\nL450: \nL451: Since $U_{\\eta}$ preserves $H_{A_{2}}+H_{B_{2}}$, it preserves this state:\nL452: \nL453:  | $$U_{\\eta}(\\tau_{2}\\otimes\\tau_{2})U_{\\eta}^{\\dagger}=(\\tau_{2})_{G_{C}}\\otimes(\\tau_{2})_{G_{E}}.$$  |  | (62)\nL454: \nL455: In particular, the auxiliary outputs are independent and $L_{\\eta}(A_{2},B_{2})=mg(2)-\\eta mg(2)-(1-\\eta)mg(2)=0$.\nL456: ##### 2. Apply the doubling identity.\nL457: \nL458: Use the first circuit order in Fig. cite64†1 (a), with $M=A_{+}B_{+}$. Lemma cite70†3 , at $p=\\eta$, gives\nL459: \nL460:  | $$0=L_{\\eta}(A_{+},B_{+})+L_{\\eta}(A_{-},B_{-}\\mid M)+I(C_{-};E_{+}\\mid C_{+})_{\\Xi}.$$  |  | (63)\nL461: \nL462: The lemma verifies the conditional-independence hypothesis needed for the conditional deficit. All three terms are nonnegative, hence\nL463: \nL464:  | $$I(C_{-};E_{+}\\mid C_{+})_{\\Xi}=0.$$  |  | (64)\nL465: ##### 3. Read the circuit as local thermal noise.\nL466: \nL467: By the commutation of the two kinds of beam splitters proved in Lemma cite70†3 , the second circuit order has the same four-output state $\\Xi$. Immediately after the $\\eta$-beam splitters, the state is\nL468: \nL469:  | $$\\omega_{CE}\\otimes(\\tau_{2})_{G_{C}}\\otimes(\\tau_{2})_{G_{E}}.$$  |  | (65)\nL470: \nL471: Mix $E,G_{E}$ on the balanced beam splitter and retain $J:=E_{+}$. The state before mixing $C,G_{C}$ is\nL472:  | $$\\sigma_{CJ}\\otimes(\\tau_{2})_{G_{C}},\\qquad\\sigma_{CJ}=(\\operatorname{id}_{C}\\otimes\\Phi)(\\omega_{CE}).$$  |  | (66)\nL473: \nL474: The auxiliary $G_{C}$ is independent of the entire pair $CJ$ because it is a separate factor in (cite102†65 ). After the remaining balanced beam splitter, the $C_{+}J$ marginal is\nL475: \nL476:  | $$\\Xi_{C_{+}J}=(\\Phi\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{J})(\\sigma_{CJ}).$$  |  | (67)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquality in the Bosonic Quantum Entropy Power Inequality (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.40061v1)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":620}); Total lines: 812\nL603:  | $$\\chi_{\\rho^{\\boxplus n}}(\\xi)=\\chi_{\\rho}(\\xi/\\sqrt{n})^{n},\\qquad\\xi\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2m}.$$  |  | (86)\nL604: This also shows that the retained state is independent of the chosen orthogonal completion. The state $\\rho^{\\boxplus n}$ is the $n$-fold symmetric quantum convolution of $\\rho$ studied in [cite68†19 , cite74†23 , cite78†27 ]; the characteristic function (cite119†86 ) shows that it coincides with the $n$-fold convolution defined there. For $n=2$ it is $\\mathcal{B}_{1/2}(\\rho\\otimes\\rho)$, and repeated balanced self-convolution gives $\\rho^{\\boxplus 2^{k}}$ after $k$ steps.\nL605: If $\\rho$ has displacement $d$ and covariance $V$, then $\\rho^{\\boxplus n}$ has displacement $\\sqrt{n}\\,d$ and covariance $V$. In particular, for a centered state, all these convolutions have the same oscillator energy\nL606: \nL607:  | $$\\mathsf{E}=\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho H_{m}=\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V-m}{2}.$$  |  | (87)\nL608: \nL609: This is why centering precedes the limit argument.\nL610: \nL611: Winter’s Lemma 15 [cite69†20 ] yields the explicit bound\nL612:  | $$|S(\\rho)-S(\\sigma)|\\leq 2\\epsilon m\\,g\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathsf{E}}{m\\epsilon}\\right)+h_{2}(\\epsilon),$$  |  | (88)\nL613: \nL614: where $\\epsilon=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left\\lVert\\rho-\\sigma\\right\\rVert_{1}\\in(0,1]$ and both oscillator energies are at most $\\mathsf{E}$. Here $h_{2}(t)=-t\\log t-(1-t)\\log(1-t)$. The right side tends to zero with $\\epsilon$. The following lemmas record the precise continuity and limit statements.\nL615: ###### Lemma 11 (Entropy continuity under an oscillator energy bound).\nL616: \nL617: Let $\\omega_{j}$ and $\\omega$ be $m$-mode states such that\nL618: \nL619:  | $$\\left\\lVert\\omega_{j}-\\omega\\right\\rVert_{1}\\longrightarrow 0,\\qquad\\max\\{\\operatorname{Tr}\\omega_{j}H_{m},\\operatorname{Tr}\\omega H_{m}\\}\\leq\\mathsf{E}<\\infty.$$  |\nL620: \nL621: Then $S(\\omega_{j})\\to S(\\omega)$.\nL622: \nL623: ###### Proof.\nL624: \nL625: It follows directly from (cite120†88 ). ∎\nL626: ###### Lemma 12 (Finite-energy convolution central limit theorem).\nL627: \nL628: Let $\\rho$ be a centered $m$-mode state of finite energy and covariance $V$. Let $\\gamma_{V}$ be the centered Gaussian state with covariance $V$. Then\nL629: \nL630:  | $$\\left\\lVert\\rho^{\\boxplus n}-\\gamma_{V}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\longrightarrow 0,\\qquad S(\\rho^{\\boxplus n})\\longrightarrow S(\\gamma_{V}).$$  |  | (89)\nL631: ###### Proof.\nL632: \nL633: The finite-energy assumption gives all second moments. The trace-norm convergence is the Cushen–Hudson theorem in the form of [cite68†19 , Theorem 5]; quantitative rates under higher moments are given in [cite68†19 , cite74†23 ]. Centering and (cite121†87 ) keep every iterate, and its Gaussian limit, in the same energy ball. Then Lemma cite122†11 gives the desired entropy convergence. ∎\nL634: \nL635: ### 4.4 Proof of Theorem cite123†2 L636: ###### Proof of Theorem cite123†2 .\nL637: \nL638: Different displacements of the inputs do not alter any entropy, hence we can assume the displacements are all $0$. Fix $p=p_{*}$ from the original centered pair. Exponential equality means $F_{p}(A,B)=0$. For $X=A,B,C$ and each integer $k\\geq 0$, let $\\rho_{X}^{(k)}=\\rho_{X}^{\\boxplus 2^{k}}$. Apply Lemma cite70†3 to two independent copies of $(\\rho_{A}^{(k)},\\rho_{B}^{(k)})$. Its plus pair is the next iterate, so\nL639:  | $$0\\leq F_{p}(A^{(k+1)},B^{(k+1)})\\leq 2F_{p}(A^{(k)},B^{(k)}).$$  |  | (90)\nL640: \nL641: The discarded terms are the conditional deficit and a CMI. The conditional qEPI is used at this fixed $p=p_{*}$, which need not yet equal $\\eta$. Since the initial deficit is zero, induction gives\nL642: \nL643:  | $$F_{p}(A^{(k)},B^{(k)})=0\\qquad(k\\geq 0).$$  |  | (91)\nL644: \nL645: That the output of the iterated pair is $\\rho_{C}^{(k)}$ follows from commutation of the two kinds of mixing. The same $p$ is retained for all $k$.\nL646: For each $X=A,B,C$, the definition of the $n$-fold convolution and independence give $d_{X^{(k)}}=0$ and $V_{X^{(k)}}=V_{X}$. Here $V_{C}=\\eta V_{A}+(1-\\eta)V_{B}$ is fixed as well. By (cite73†8 ), each of the three sequences has its own fixed finite energy. Lemma cite124†12 therefore gives trace-norm convergence to $\\gamma_{V_{A}}$, $\\gamma_{V_{B}}$, and $\\gamma_{V_{C}}$, respectively, and Lemma cite122†11 gives convergence of all three entropies.\nL647: The coefficient $p$ and the constant $md(p\\|\\eta)$ remain unchanged. We may consequently take the limit in the three finite entropy terms of (cite125†91 ), obtaining\nL648:  | $$F_{p}(\\gamma_{V_{A}},\\gamma_{V_{B}})=0.$$  |  | (92)\nL649: \nL650: Lemma cite126†10 yields $V_{A}=V_{B}$ and $p=p_{*}=\\eta$. The formula for $p_{*}$ then gives $S(A)=S(B)$, and $L_{\\eta}(A,B)=F_{\\eta}(A,B)=0$. Theorem cite59†1 proves Gaussianity.\nL651: \nL652: Conversely, matched-covariance Gaussian inputs have $S(C)=S(A)=S(B)$ and satisfy exponential equality directly. ∎\nL653: ## 5 Discussion\nL654: \nL655: The equality condition for the bosonic qEPI can be expressed entirely in terms of the two output systems: an independent finite-energy pair attains either entropy inequality precisely when its beam-splitter outputs are independent. The Gaussian characterization then follows from the quantum Darmois–Skitovich theorem. The thermal auxiliary is what connects an entropy condition on one output to this structural condition on both outputs.\nL656: The method also separates two roles of Gaussian states. The auxiliary thermal states are introduced at a fixed finite energy to obtain strict information loss. The Gaussian limit in the exponential proof serves a different purpose: it determines the affine weight and the input covariances. The linear equality proof is complete before that limit argument is used.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1902.05298v3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
